%% Copyright (C) 2026 Gil Hauer
%% SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-3.0-only
%% Audience: Python readers learning why the sieve reduces enumeration cost.
%% Question: How does elapsed time grow when the upper limit increases?
%% Local benchmark: median of seven runs, rounded to 0.001 ms.
%% Inputs are equally spaced: 50000, 100000, ..., 400000.
%% Direct labels, proportional axes, restrained rules, and no decoration follow
%% Tufte's emphasis on graphical integrity and data-ink:
%% https://www.edwardtufte.com/book/the-visual-display-of-quantitative-information/
%% Solid/dashed lines remain distinguishable in monochrome.
xychart
    title "Time to find every prime up to the limit"
    accTitle: Trial division and sieve runtime as the upper limit increases
    accDescr: Median of seven local runs at each limit. From 50000 to 400000, trial division rises from 26.279 to 576.511 milliseconds; the sieve rises from 6.128 to 56.206 milliseconds. Trial division is solid and the sieve is dashed. Lines connect measurements, not a fitted model.
    x-axis "Upper limit" 50000 --> 400000
    y-axis "Median elapsed time (ms)" 0 --> 650
    line [26.279, 59.767, 127.829, 172.554, 250.603, 325.802, 422.094 "Trial division", 576.511]
    line [6.128, 9.437, 18.253, 20.315, 31.652, 31.453, 41.685 "Sieve", 56.206]
